<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stainmore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stainmore Pass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stainmore is a high pass across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> between the Eden valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> and Teesdale, followed by a Roman road and the modern A66. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked a frontier of the Northumbrian and Cumbrian lands, and the stump of the Rey Cross stands on the summit. According to later tradition, the last Norse king of York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>, was killed there in 954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stainmore.jpg" alt="Stainmore" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANDREW SMITH · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mousegill_with_Great_Knipe_in_the_background_-_geograph.org.uk_-_598472.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stainmore is a remote area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, County Durham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>. It is drained to the west by the River Belah and to the east by the River Balder, Deepdale Beck and the River Greta. The Roman road from Bowes to Brough, the Stainmore Railway and the roads have all used the low, broad saddle between higher hills known as the Stainmore Gap; the railway's summit lay at around 420 metres above sea level.</p><p>The Gap follows the Stainmore Summit Fault, and during one or more glacial periods it carried ice from the Lake District eastwards. The area gives its name to the Stainmore Trough, a geological structure of the Carboniferous period lying between the Alston Block and the Askrigg Block. Bowes Moor is a Site of Special Scientific Interest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name, meaning stony moor, is first attested in a document of about 990 as Stanmoir. It appears as Stanmore in the Charter Rolls of the reign of Henry II and as Staynmor in the Placita de Quo Warranto of 1292. According to the chronicler Roger of Wendover, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>, recently expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, was betrayed and killed at Stainmore, and some historians believe this happened in a great battle.</p><p>A Roman marching camp at Rey Cross is a Scheduled Monument. Immediately east of it stands the Rey Cross itself, also called Rere Cross, both a Scheduled Monument and a Listed Building. St Stephen's church at South Stainmore was built by Cuthbert Buckell in 1600 and rebuilt in 1842–1843 by Henry Tufton, 11th Earl of Thanet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Stainmore is a civil parish in Westmorland and Furness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, including the villages of North Stainmore and South Stainmore, with 264 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Stainmore Forest stretches east into County Durham towards Bowes. The ecclesiastical parish of Brough with Stainmore has two churches, St Michael's at Brough under Stainmore and St Stephen's.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stainmore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last of them, Eric Bloodaxe, was expelled in 954, traditionally dying at <b>Stainmore</b>, and York and the rest of Northumbria were absorbed into the Kingdom of England, with the north governed by earls of the house of Bamburgh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
